The query editor - the state machine
description: the breadcrumb, the pointer pick cells, and the tag picker - one machine in three hosts
- >
  Each subchapter is a **state** of the editable breadcrumb - the topbar's locator and query
  editor in one (a permanent row of smart cells, one per query segment). Unlike the yamlover
  editor's, this machine is ALSO executable code - `tools/server/src/client/breadcrumb-machine.ts`
  is the pure reducer these states compile to, and `breadcrumb-machine.test.ts` runs the table.
  [The projectional editor](*::server:editor) is the prose architecture doc for how this machine
  hosts the breadcrumb, the pointer cell, and the tag picker.
- >
  **Conventions.** A SEGMENT is one colon-separated step of the query - a key (`pets[1]` includes
  its index suffixes), a wildcard (`?`, `-`), descent (`...`), an uplink (`..`, `?..`), a value
  test (`>10`, `=text`) or a meta test (`!!<type: integer>`). While any cell is focused the TOC
  FILTERS live (matches + all their ancestors, expanded down to the matches, no row selected); the
  dropdown under the cell offers the context's REAL children as true TOC rows plus the operator
  hints. Hints are never validators - free-typed text always stands, and the dropdown arms only on
  an explicit ArrowDown or hover (nothing armed = Enter commits the typed query). TAB is the
  ACCEPT key: it takes the armed candidate, or the FIRST one when nothing is armed. The dropdown
  is PORTALED to the body at fixed coordinates under the cell, so no ancestor popup or scrolling
  pane can clip it. A query that stops parsing mid-edit keeps the LAST GOOD filter on screen and
  rings the cell red - it never blocks. Candidate and filter fetches are debounced and
  sequence-guarded; a stale arrival is dropped.
- >
  **HOST MODES.** The SAME machine drives every query-cell surface, in one of two modes. BROWSE -
  the breadcrumb: selecting means navigating, a committed query keeps the `filtered` browsing
  state, and the scope is fixed at the document root. PICK - a selection host: selecting means
  committing ONE node path (the query REDUCES to a path - a pointer, never a matcher), `filtered`
  is unreachable, and the SCOPE LADDER is the user's: bare (current scope), `:` document, `::`
  project, `:::` world. In pick mode a TOC click is an INSERTION, not a commit. The TOC filter is
  ONE shared surface: a session owner holds it, and a host that starts editing EVICTS the previous
  owner. The append cell (`click_tail` / F4) REUSES an already-empty trailing cell, never doubles
  it.
- >
  **THE THREE HOSTS.** (1) The BREADCRUMB - browse mode, this chapter's main table. (2) The yed
  editor's REFERENCE CELL (`*` in a hole, and the atom's pick retarget) - pick mode; its outer
  states live in [the yamlover editor machine](*::server:yamlover-editor) (`pointer_entry` /
  `pointer_pick_editing` / `atom_focused`), which delegate the inner grammar here through the cell
  registry's `holePick`/pointer seam. (3) The TAG PICKER (the annotation popup's search row) -
  pick mode, opening at the PROJECT scope with the caret already in the trailing cell, cells
  seeded `...` + an empty cell so a bare typed name spells the find-anywhere query `:: ...:
  <name>`; Enter commits the first match, and a TOC row click TOGGLES the node as the annotation
  ref (already applied -> it unapplies), while Enter ASSIGNS the typed path (already applied -> a
  satisfied no-op). Create-on-miss births a named tag from a bare unmatched name; any OTHER miss
  keeps the cells ringed and editable, never a modal error ([annotations](*::annotations)).
- >
  **THE SEGMENT RULE.** `:` and `[` are STRUCTURE, not text. Typing `:` splits the cell at the
  caret - the tail becomes the next cell. Typing `[` projects the closing `]` with the caret in
  between; `]` jumps OVER the projected closer; Backspace between an empty projected pair
  dismantles it. An index belongs to the segment BEFORE it - `[` in an EMPTY cell folds into the
  previous cell (`pets` `:` `[` `1` spells `pets[1]`, never `pets: [1]`). For the FIRST cell the
  previous segment is the ROOT itself. Backspace at a cell's start (or Delete at a cell's end)
  deletes the `:` boundary - the neighbouring cells merge with the caret at the junction.
- >
  **THE REGRESSION RULE.** Leaving the editor always lands somewhere honest: selecting a node that
  MATCHES the query keeps the query (the `filtered` state - cells intact, TOC still filtered);
  selecting anything else regresses the breadcrumb to that node's plain path; Escape restores the
  current path's cells and the unfiltered TOC.
- >
  **Pseudo-states.** `nop`, `error_ring`, `focus_prev`, `focus_next` follow the yamlover editor's:
  consume-and-stay, red-ring-and-stay, and the two focus-walk actions landing in the adjacent
  cell's focused state.
